  {
    "episode_id": 91279,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91279.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 10,
    "turn_text": "Well, I did have a role. And first, I want to say that we have a good team here in Iowa. our corn growers and our soybean association and when we're trading livestock, our livestock associations and farm beer are all great at promoting trade and also our Department of Economic Development links this together. This was a national deal that we signed in Washington day before yesterday and I came here to ratify it in Iowa today down in the capital with the lieutenant governor and a whole group of Taiwanese dignitaries and representatives from corn and soybeans. But this agreement is something that has been working for a long time. And then from my standpoint, I pay a lot of attention to our foreign relations and our network with the representatives of foreign countries. I've sent at least six of my staff people over to Taiwan. I went there a couple of years ago to represent the United States and the celebration of the victory, our victory together, in World War II, with as the guest of President Ma at the time. They have a new president now, President Tsai. And the first woman president of Taiwan, I met with her, we had an engaging conversation, met with agriculture, economic, defense foreign affairs, met with the president of the legislature, all to help tie down this agreement that turns out to be $2.8 billion. Most of it comes to Iowa, although there's some weak part of the agreement and a little bit will come from a few other states. Well, probably over $2 billion that would be a part of the Iowa transaction.",
