  {
    "episode_id": 91279,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91279.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 19,
    "turn_text": "Well, I don't know if I can put it into perspective. I call it a really big deal, and I don't want to imply that this is the end of it all. It's not just the opening round, but it's an important, significant, high milestone That was valuable enough that they sent a large delegation they pulled in on a bus. And that's how much they care about this. And here's a big deal from dollars and cents standpoint. We've produced more than we can ever consume. And the President has been negotiating trade, and he's worried about trade deficits. But what agriculture is, it produces the component that gives us a surplus in some of these trade agreements that we have. that's that's one of the reasons that for most all of that money comes into aio i'll just say this way and i can i can defend it more easily the majority of that money comes into aio and it gets that in aio of multiple times and it's our family farms through the beneficiaries of this and that's why it's it's a big deal for aio but i want to talk about how big a deal it is for taiwan and that's because there they are you know they've they became a country and i call them a country although they're not that's really recognized as a sovereign nation by many countries in the world but uh... they are their sovereignty was started to get hammered out nineteen forty nine when they went across the straits to get away from the mark this mouthy tongue and a shankah shak led them over there they formed their nation on the idea of freedom in opposition to communism so they have a lot of the same spirit we have and this strengthens that spirit time and in and then their defense uh... that they used to be just simply they could stand off the mainland chinese militarily but now the mainland china has built such a powerful military force the defense for taiwan is what they can do militarily plus the strength of their international relationships plus the strength of their economy and their trading agreements that they have in the interchange we have with taiwan e students twenty one thousand of them in the united states studying right now All of those relationships, plus Taiwan's trade with the mainland, all strengthens their components of their national defense today, deterrent factors, and instead is combined. That's the best they have. So when they reach a deal like this with Iowa, they have about three or four more reasons to be happy about it than we do. And that's why their smiles are even bigger than ours at the signing",
